    "knowledge_point": "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function",
    "problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2-x}?$",
    "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-x\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ne 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne 2\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $x=2$ and shade *right* and *left* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098447/assets/2371c9724d66e0f7d74c45e173f9625a986615185c10d55c029976baa82d0877.png)",
